From f50867cc142ad03ec896b65d99536cf34f697219 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 06:57:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Judge an arriving derivation against what this database
 already knows

memory.derived events do not come only from this instance: they arrive from
the stream, during a rebuild, from a second writer, or late after a
partition. The sender derived them from evidence it believed active. This
database may already know better.

Two cases it can decide locally, and did not:

- A known direct parent that is no longer active. The conclusion was being
  admitted as active support for evidence that had been retracted, and
  nothing downstream could tell it apart from one just derived. It is now
  kept, with its lineage, but retracted, and whatever already depends on it
  (an out-of-order child that arrived first) is retired the same way a local
  retraction retires dependents.

- A version older than the one already standing. supersedes was honoured on
  arrival order, so a stale version delivered late retired the newer one.
  Producer time decides instead; the stale arrival is kept as history,
  superseded by the current version. Equal producer time still supersedes,
  which is what two versions computed in the same second produce, so the
  previous behaviour survives at the boundary.

Timestamps are compared as instants, not strings: two writers emit producer
time with their own UTC offsets. A timestamp that cannot be parsed counts as
not newer, so an undatable version never displaces a datable one.

Deliberately unchanged: a parent this database has never seen cannot be
judged from here and is still admitted, and the retired dependents are not
re-evaluated on remaining evidence the way a local retraction re-evaluates --
those conclusions were computed elsewhere, and re-deriving them here would
invent support this instance cannot account for.

tests/derived_freshness.rs covers all of it against real SQLite through
apply_event. Before the change: 1 passed, 2 failed (baseline 64027cb,
service.rs sha256 767e2101e08e489225b50a5ce1b11428dad80cc5bfdbdf2a143e4cf37b974027).
After: 4 passed. Whole crate: 91 passed, 0 failed -- but 9 of those are the
jetstream tests, which self-skip without a nats-server binary, so the
delayed-derivation path is still untested over NATS.

